Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and plan of care, and the beneficiary must work with the multidisciplinary team to identify those needs. The Multidisciplinary Team’s documentation must show the need for the modification, relate it to the service plan, and include specific supporting records such as recommendations, cost information, and other required records. Current source: [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), effective Sept. 1, 2025; no amendments were returned for this document.

## Home modification requirements under CAP/DA

The policy starts from the beneficiary’s obligation to collaborate with the team: the beneficiary agrees to “work collaboratively with my multidisciplinary team to identify my needs for equipment, modification, technology, training and education and goods and services.” [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

For coverage, equipment/modification/technology services must be directed to one of these purposes:

- improve, maximize, or enhance the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, or independence;
- improve integration into the community;
- improve the beneficiary’s environmental and community accessibility; or
- address the beneficiary’s 24-hour, 7-day coverage concerns. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy’s home-modification examples include, among other things:

- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker accessibility;
- medically necessary emergency egress modifications;
- bathroom modifications for accessibility;
- bedroom modifications for hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen modifications to improve accessibility;
- certain flooring for ease of ambulation;
- door handle or door modifications; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space when removed or damaged during an approved modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The location rules in the policy say home modifications may be made to:

- a primary private residence where the beneficiary lives and that is owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence when the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence when portable modifications are allowed by the owner. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The policy also includes some important limits. CAP/DA does not cover items:

- not of direct medical or remedial benefit to the beneficiary;
- covered by Medicaid State Plan DME, orthotics, prosthetics, or home health supplies;
- recreational in nature;
- of general utility to a nondisabled person; or
- not approved in the person-centered service plan where approval is required. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Documentation the Multidisciplinary Team must provide

For equipment, modification, technology, and supplies, § 5.9.1 requires:

- a recommendation by the MDT identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested home modification;
- the recommendation must state the cost of the item required;
- a statement of need identified in the service plan;
- evidence of medical need recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- itemized shipping costs in the proposal request;
- any other information required for the specific equipment or modification requested;
- when quotes are required for a purchase, adaptation, or modification, NC Medicaid determines how many quotes are required; and
- in special circumstances, NC Medicaid or its designee determines which professionals must provide written recommendations. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The same section also requires:

- a plan for how the beneficiary and family will be trained to use the equipment; and
- documentation by the case manager that training was completed, signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

Additional supporting documentation requirements in the policy include:

- a comprehensive Multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a copy of the physician’s order, when applicable;
- a recommendation from an appropriate professional identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the requested equipment, supply, adaptation, or modification;
- the estimated life of the equipment and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- an invoice from the supplier showing the date provided, cost, and related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Bottom line

For a CAP/DA home modification, the MDT must document that the modification is needed for the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, accessibility, or round-the-clock care needs and that it is reflected in the service plan. The documentation should include the MDT recommendation, statement of need, medical-necessity support when applicable, cost information, required quotes if requested by Medicaid, and related supporting records such as the declaration-of-need assessment, physician order if applicable, and supplier invoice. If you want, I can turn this into a short attorney checklist or prior-approval document checklist.

Physicians should provide Medicaid-covered fluoride varnish **from tooth eruption until the child is 3½ years old**. NC Medicaid also limits billing for physician-applied fluoride varnish to beneficiaries **under 3½ years of age**, **once every 60 calendar days**, and **no more than six times before the child reaches 3½**. It must be billed with the oral evaluation code D0145. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment) (effective Dec. 4, 2019), §§ 7.2, 5.3.2.

## Oral screening requirements

NC Medicaid’s physician fluoride varnish policy requires:

- **Early caries screening** and detection of notable findings, including obvious hard- and soft-tissue pathology in the oral cavity, using **a dental mirror and directed light**;
- **Counseling and educational materials** on good oral hygiene and nutrition for children;
- **Prescribing a fluoride supplement, if indicated**, following American Academy of Pediatrics guidelines;
- **Application of fluoride varnish to all erupted primary teeth**, beginning at tooth eruption until age 3½. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), § 7.2.

## Required documentation in the health record

The beneficiary’s health record must document all of the following:

- **An oral evaluation and any notable findings**;
- **Preventive oral health and dietary counseling with the primary caregiver**;
- **Application of fluoride varnish**; and
- **Referral to a dentist, if appropriate**. [Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment), § 7.2.

