Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet the certification standards in Policy 12B and continue to meet them over time; a provider can be decertified for a broad set of compliance, staffing, training, billing, documentation, and quality failures. The main current source is Policy 12B, effective January 15, 2020, and I checked amendments for that document and the recertification checklist; neither showed a later amending notice in the corpus. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment); [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid requires an HIV case management provider to:

- have a documented successful record of **3 years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** under the policy’s staff qualification rules;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** under the policy’s supervisor qualification rules;
- enroll **each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a **quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed ownership/name/address/telephone/business dissolution changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**; and
- achieve **national accreditation within 1 year of Medicaid enrollment** from one of the listed accrediting bodies: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Recertification-related submission requirements

The HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist shows that recertification materials must address, at minimum:

- one physical location per application;
- a title page and signed recertification checklist;
- complete agency demographic/contact information;
- all case managers listed with dates of employment;
- all case management supervisors listed with dates of employment;
- all counties where HIV case management services are provided;
- all services the agency provides;
- operating hours and the emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- frequency of satisfaction surveys and how results are used; and
- numbers of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

## When a provider can be decertified

Policy 12B says an HIV case management provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing** practices;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a written **plan of correction** for unmet program requirements, make recommended corrections, or both, within **30 calendar days**;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the policy’s qualification criteria;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the time required by policy, NC Medicaid, or its designated contractor;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing as required by the policy;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of agency name, director, ownership, mailing address, or telephone changes, resulting in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, Medicaid state plan requirements, and governing policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **4 months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the policy’s **certification requirements**. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

## Practical bottom line

For legal or compliance purposes, the decertification standard is not limited to fraud or serious misconduct. A provider can also lose certification for operational failures such as unqualified staff, missed training/CEUs, inadequate supervision, deficient documentation, failure to support billing, missing notices to Medicaid, or failure to maintain accreditation/certification requirements. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment)

